WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:06.400
Hello! In today's Bug Bounty Report Explained,&nbsp;
you will learn how it was possible to hack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:12.560
Facebook in not one but three different ways.&nbsp;
Those vulnerabilities were submitted across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:18.480
two reports for which the researcher,&nbsp;
Alaa Abdulridha, got $55,000 in total.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:22.480
Links to the original blogposts&nbsp;
are in the description.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:30.720
Enjoy! So there were two blogposts by the same&nbsp;
author. In part one, he found an application

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:38.640
legal.tapprd.facebook.com. There was&nbsp;
no registration but he found a hidden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:46.080
endpoint /savepassword which allowed him to take&nbsp;
over arbitrary account. From javascript files&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:51.040
he prepared a valid body and then&nbsp;
brute-forced the list of usernames.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:58.640
The endpoint allowed changing anyone's password,&nbsp;
provided that we know their username. So it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:06.240
complete account takeover. During his brute&nbsp;
force he hit one correct username to prepare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:15.840
the proof of concept. He reported the bug and got&nbsp;
$7,500 for it. But he wanted more. His idea was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:22.480
to test session management. He had to&nbsp;
look into his burp history to see what was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:29.680
going on when he was testing for the first&nbsp;
vulnerability. He noticed ASPXAUTH cookie.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:37.760
It's a default cookie name from standard .NET's&nbsp;
FormAuthentication class. It works this way:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:43.600
there is this thing called ticket which contains&nbsp;
your data that you want to be stored inside the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:50.560
cookie. Usually, it's data that can identify&nbsp;
the user, for example username or user id.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.240 --> 00:02:00.880
First, the ticket in cleartext is signed using&nbsp;
SHA-1 algorithm. Then, the whole ticket along&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:09.520
with the signature is encrypted and then the&nbsp;
encrypted block is signed again using SHA-1. SHA-1&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:18.560
is a default algorithm for signing while AES is a&nbsp;
standard encryption algorithm. The developer must&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:26.640
set at least two keys: validationKey for signing&nbsp;
and decryptionKey for encryption and decryption&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:33.920
of the data. The typical flow looks like this:&nbsp;
when you successfully log into the application,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:41.280
some of your data is being put in this cookie&nbsp;
and this cookie is sent with your every request.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:46.160
And upon every request the application&nbsp;
must validate the first signature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:53.600
decrypt the cookie and validate the second&nbsp;
signature. At the end, it decodes your username&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.600 --> 00:03:00.640
or user id from the ticket and it confirms&nbsp;
that it's actually you. If something goes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:08.880
wrong along the way, you are not authenticated.&nbsp;
Keys are sufficiently strong so that the cookie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:18.480
is not brute-forceable. There is also no known&nbsp;
way to disclose those keys. But the bughunter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:24.720
assumed that this application is vulnerable&nbsp;
once he saw this cookie name. Why was that?

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:34.720
The documentation tells you to just provide the&nbsp;
username. No user id, no application id, nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:42.080
like this. And what's important, your username&nbsp;
is shared across many different applications.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:49.520
Is that a problem? It's not until you use&nbsp;
different keys for different websites.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:58.720
And according to the blogpost only 20% of&nbsp;
applications do this properly and 80% of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:05.760
are vulnerable. I find this number a bit hard&nbsp;
to believe but I have never myself tested for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:14.160
this vulnerability. The attack looks like this:&nbsp;
you have application 1 to which you can't log in.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:24.080
It uses ASPXAUTH cookie to identify the user.&nbsp;
Then, you find the second application that must&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:30.000
also use the ASPXAUTH cookie, but&nbsp;
this website must allow registration.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:37.920
Those applications must be related - running&nbsp;
on the same server. For this attack to work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:44.160
you must know the username from user from&nbsp;
app 1 whose account you will take over.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:51.360
In our case, the bughunter brute-forced&nbsp;
the username during the first attack.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:58.240
Now, if a validationKey and decryptionKey&nbsp;
are shared across those two applications&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:06.480
you can take the victim's login from app 1 and&nbsp;
sign up using this username to up to app 2.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:13.120
After logging in, app 2 will sign your cookie&nbsp;
and encrypt it and then sign it once again.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:21.680
Then, you copy this cookie from app&nbsp;
2 to app 1. App 1 will successfully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:26.640
validate and decrypt the cookie, because&nbsp;
keys are shared between those applications.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:35.840
Then, the application will take the ticket which&nbsp;
was inside the cookie. There is no application id&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:44.240
that mismatches here, there is no unique user id.&nbsp;
There is only the username, so app1 logs you in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:51.920
with that username, finishing our arbitrary&nbsp;
account takeover. That's exactly what the hunter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.920 --> 00:05:59.200
did here. Unfortunately, details of the second&nbsp;
application are not disclosed in the blogpost.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.640 --> 00:06:06.800
If you are like me, right now you are wondering&nbsp;
how did this happen that developers from such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:15.440
a big company just copied the keys from one app&nbsp;
to another. But turns out they probably didn't.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:23.040
By default, keys are not created manually,&nbsp;
but auto-generated using machineKey Element.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:29.040
And those keys are derived from the&nbsp;
machine properties, so if you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:36.640
multiple websites running on the same server,&nbsp;
they will have the same keys. To avoid that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.640 --> 00:06:44.240
you need to use isolateApps modifier which makes&nbsp;
the key being generated using the application id.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:49.120
At least that's what one page&nbsp;
from the documentation says.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.480 --> 00:06:58.160
The other page says that it only uses virtual&nbsp;
paths. This would work if you would have one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:07.280
domain and one application would work under / path&nbsp;
and the other would work under /app2. They would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:15.200
have different virtual paths, thus different keys.&nbsp;
But in today's world it's not the most common use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.200 --> 00:07:23.840
case. More likely, your two applications will work&nbsp;
on two different ports, let's say 8080 and 8081.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:31.280
Then, you would have a reverse proxy that&nbsp;
routes the traffic from one domain to port 8080&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:39.600
and the traffic to other domain to port 8081.&nbsp;
The customer only uses port 443 on both domains.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:47.520
But coming back to our app isolation, both of them&nbsp;
in such case would have / as their virtual path.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.320 --> 00:07:54.720
So if isolate apps modifier was used, keys&nbsp;
will still be exactly the same across those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.720 --> 00:08:02.320
applications. As this is the default setting I'd&nbsp;
assume that's what happened with Facebook. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.320 --> 00:08:10.160
fix for that is changing isolateApps modifier&nbsp;
to isolateByAppId which actually uses the app&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.160 --> 00:08:17.760
id. This is a typical example of insecure by&nbsp;
default and I wouldn't even put much blame on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.760 --> 00:08:24.960
the developers - no way they could see this coming&nbsp;
reading this documentation. Even for me, when I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:31.360
knew exactly what am I looking for, it took a few&nbsp;
hours to dig deep to find all this information.

00:08:33.440 --> 00:08:42.240
So he could get another $7,500 for that, but it&nbsp;
wasn't enough. This time, he wanted to go further.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.800 --> 00:08:49.360
He asked for permission to test for SSRFs because&nbsp;
there was a functionality that brought his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:57.360
attention: API trigger. It was pretty much SSRF&nbsp;
by design - you could make requests anywhere.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.080 --> 00:09:06.400
Facebook gave him green light to test for this. It&nbsp;
even has an internal endpoint that you can request&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.400 --> 00:09:14.240
to show the proof of concept of your SSRF. So&nbsp;
he created a custom script in some editor that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:21.600
allowed him to make requests to arbitrary URLs,&nbsp;
but also with arbitrary headers and arbitrary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:29.280
methods. It's important, because Facebook SSRF&nbsp;
guidelines say that for SSRF when you can read&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.280 --> 00:09:37.120
responses, you get $40,000 but if you can control&nbsp;
the whole request, including headers, you get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.120 --> 00:09:47.440
$5,000 bonus. In total, he got $54,800 for those&nbsp;
two reports. Quite a good payout if you ask me.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.080 --> 00:09:55.680
And that's it. I myself didn't know about this&nbsp;
ASPXAUTH cookie before and even searching strictly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.680 --> 00:10:02.240
for that I didn't find many sources about it.&nbsp;
Considering that FormAuthentication class is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.240 --> 00:10:08.720
a part .NET's standard security module, it's&nbsp;
probably quite common. So if you also didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.720 --> 00:10:15.120
know about this, leave a like under this video.&nbsp;
Also, sign up for my mailing list to be the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:21.360
to know about any of my future projects.&nbsp;
For now, thank you for watching and goodbye!

